{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1788351399,"nonce":"WnwjlEctS3Jj6Kze1jmqerEKVeM=","target":"a1644d677bbb6393ade7933367e947f7d9e90b9f1b19d2ed1d5fc38c3cdde61a","message":"Run `m1-20260901`: the freezer was placed as a layout, built, and **never enclosed**. `room-at` on its interior returned `outdoors: true, cells: 60082` — the great outdoors — for the rest of the run. The colony therefore had no larder, ran hand-to-mouth into winter, and starved. This is one of the three defects named as the cause of the wipe.\n\nThe agent could not see it. `room-at` answers for one cell, honestly, and nothing connects a *placed layout that was supposed to be a room* to the question *is it a room yet*. Finding this out costs a cell-by-cell interrogation the agent has no reason to run on a layout that reported `placed` with zero blockers.\n\n\nThe original filing (and `next-run.md` B-3 as first written) named only the first. Established against the decompiled source before dispatch; DESIGN.md decisions log, commit `8c8680a`.\n\n**1. Not enclosed.** `Verse/Room.cs`:\n\n```csharp\npublic bool ProperRoom {\n  get {\n    if (TouchesMapEdge) return false;\n    for (int i = 0; i \u003c districts.Count; i++)\n      if (districts[i].RegionType == RegionType.Normal) return true;\n    return false;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nA layout that never closed leaks into the map-wide outdoor room, which touches the map edge, so `ProperRoom` is false. This is the `outdoors: true, cells: 60082` the run measured — a wall with a hole in it.\n\n**2. Enclosed but thermally outdoors.**\n\n```csharp\npublic bool UsesOutdoorTemperature =\u003e\n    TouchesMapEdge || OpenRoofCount \u003e= Mathf.CeilToInt(CellCount * 0.25f);\n```\n\nA sealed room missing a quarter of its roof sits on the **outdoor temperature with `ProperRoom` still true**. For a freezer that is the same dead colony by a different mechanism, and **a check reporting only `ProperRoom` would pass it clean.**\n\n`PsychologicallyOutdoors` is a third reading with different thresholds, mood-facing, and is *not* the enclosure question. Do not conflate the three.\n\n\n- **The layout-to-cells link.** `LayoutVerbs.Layouts.Open(...)` records a `CellRect` per placed layout and it survives across days — the run called `construction {layout_id:\"ly-1\"}` successfully on day 40+. Intended interior cells are that rect minus the cells occupied by declared `Wall`/`Door` elements.\n- **The intended roof.** The IR's own `roof` mask, which every shipped template in `templates/` already carries.\n\nSo naming the gap is a **comparison, not a search**: a declared `Wall`/`Door` cell with no edifice standing (or carrying an unbuilt blueprint or frame) is the hole; a declared roofed cell that is unroofed is the thermal hole. No flood fill, no pathfinding. Call the game's members and cite them, per the standing \"the gate lives in the widget\" rule.\n\nOne nuance that will bite an implementer: **a door does not break enclosure, an unbuilt door does.** The gap check keys on what stands at the cell, not on the declared def's type.\n\n\n1. `construction {layout_id:\"ly-N\"}` reports, for any layout whose IR declares an enclosed space, **both** flags: whether it is a proper room, and whether it is on outdoor temperature. Reporting only the first does not satisfy this issue.\n2. Where either fails, the report **names the gap** — the unbuilt or missing wall/door cells that leave it open, and the declared-roofed cells that are unroofed. A bare `enclosed: false` repeats the defect one level up.\n3. `rooms` lists intended-but-unenclosed layouts with both flags, so the failure is reachable from a turn-level read and not only from a caller that already suspects a specific layout.\n4. A layout still failing either flag across a day boundary is surfaced the way `f9dadc7`'s stalled blueprints are; the two roll-ups should read consistently, since an unenclosed room and a stalled blueprint are usually the same event seen from two sides.\n5. `checklists/turn.md` and `templates/freezer-kitchen.md` cite it. The template is the one that shipped this failure.\n6. `accept/\u003cid\u003e-enclosure.py` on a bench: place a room layout, leave one wall cell unbuilt, advance a day, assert the agent is told it is not enclosed and told which cell is open. Then close the wall and assert `ProperRoom` flips while a roof hole still reports outdoor temperature — the second mechanism must be provable independently of the first.\n\n\nC1 of `next-run.md`: *\"I never looked at the base after day 1.\"* One `render` on the first morning would have shown a freezer with a hole in it. The code fix is the deliverable; the playbook lesson that a numbers-only read cannot see a hole in a wall ships in the same commit.\n\n\nItem 2 of the repair round's bench list. Note that the fixture this proves it on is also the sealed roofed room that `261f2e9` phase 6 has never had — build it so both can be read off the same room.","files":null}]}